15384:     "The first two sentences hold: look_grep's only success path is `Ok(LookOutcome { printed })`, there is no error return keyed to result size, and the label is the natural place for the shortfall. The final clause — "it is forced anyway — the pending entries are already written from the full stdout by the time any bound is applied, so a refusal would have to discard a capture that already happened" — is not what observe.rs:415-479 shows. On the match branch `printed.push_str(&stdout)` runs *first*, before `by_file` is built and before any PendingEntry is constructed; and `pending::save(workspace_dir, &buf)?` is the last statement before the `Ok`. So an implementation bounding while assembling `printed` (which is how C2 describes it) reaches the bound before the entries exist and before anything is persisted, and could return `Err` without discarding any capture. Report-rather-than-refuse is a good choice here, but it is a choice, not something the code's ordering forces."
