24666:        "note": "The claim's premise is right and its inference is false. In `look_path` (observe.rs 268-336) `printed` is a separate String assembled by `printed.push_str(&shown)` at line 316 — a byte copy — and `shown` is moved into `PendingEntry.output` afterwards at line 325. Two independent Strings exist between those points, which is the same separation `look_grep` exploits. Bounding `printed` after line 316 would therefore NOT "necessarily change what is captured": the capture would be byte-identical, exactly as C2 says it is on the grep path. So the asymmetry is not "a consequence of the code's shape"; it is a choice the design is entitled to make but has not justified. This matters because C9 is an instruction to an implementer — it tells them the `look <path>` case is closed by construction, which suppresses the observation that after f3 ships, `look <path>` on a large file is still silently cut by the caller with no trace anywhere, the very defect the ticket names."
24670:        "note": "The cited range is exact: observe.rs:314-330 is `let mut printed = String::new();` … `printed.push_str(&shown);` … then `PendingEntry { … output: shown, … }`. `shown` is copied into `printed` by borrow and only moved into the entry afterwards, so the two strings are independent and a bound on `printed` cannot reach the captured output, label or key."
