C16 qualifies
The empirical clause of C16 is confirmed independently (F1: across all nine snapshot directories every extent entry's world_root is this worktree or the no-git marker), and the availability argument is unrebutted — the memo's location genuinely does not exist at capture. The qualification is on "costs nothing on the evidence available". The divergence between the two anchors is not confined to a memo citing a repository elsewhere on the machine, which is how the risks paragraph frames it. `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` answers with the *innermost* repository, so a file checked in beneath the shipping worktree as its own repository — a submodule or vendored checkout — gets a label relative to the inner root. Constructed and captured in F2: the derived label resolves, against the outer worktree the memo ships in, to a different file that exists there, contents "outer" against the observed "nested". That failure is worse than the absolute label it replaces, which fails loudly. Nothing in this repository today reaches it, so this qualifies rather than refutes — but the risk sentence should name "a repository nested inside the shipping worktree" alongside "a repository other than the one it ships in", because a reader will not read the second as covering the first.
